{"name":"Cha-Cha-Cha","hex":"#ffdb92","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cha-cha-cha","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of cha-cha."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cha-cha-cha"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cha-cha-chá (music)","blocks":[{"text":"Cha-cha-chá is a genre of Cuban music. It has been a popular dance music which developed from the danzón-mambo in the early 1950s, and became widely popular throughout the world."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cha-cha-ch%C3%A1_(music)"},{"title":"Cha-cha-cha (dance)","blocks":[{"text":"The cha-cha-chá is a dance of Cuban origin. It is danced to cha-cha-chá music introduced by the Cuban composer and violinist Enrique Jorrin in the early 1950s. This rhythm was developed from the danzón-mambo. The name of the dance is an onomatopoeia derived from the shuffling sound of the dancers' feet when they dance two consecutive quick steps that characterize the dance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Cha-Cha-Cha_Valente_Cruz_9543.JPG/330px-Cha-Cha-Cha_Valente_Cruz_9543.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cha-cha-cha_(dance)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/79/Typical_piano_accompaniment.PNG/500px-Typical_piano_accompaniment.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/79/Typical_piano_accompaniment.PNG/1280px-Typical_piano_accompaniment.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/79/Typical_piano_accompaniment.PNG/500px-Typical_piano_accompaniment.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical piano accompaniment to a cha-cha-chá (Orovio 1981:132)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATypical_piano_accompanim